<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uronarti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uronarti was a Middle Kingdom Egyptian fortress on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>. Triangular in plan to fit the island, it was part of the chain of forts guarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s southern frontier. First excavated by George Reisner's expedition in the 1920s, it is one of the best-documented of these forts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uronarti.jpg" alt="Uronarti" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 9bryan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bestock_and_Knoblauch_Final_Figure_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uronarti is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> just south of the Second Cataract, in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, in the stretch of river now called Batn-El-Hajar, the Belly of Rocks. The fortress stands high on a rocky outcrop at the northern end of the island, where it overlooks a narrow passage of the river. It belonged to a line of forts, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buhen">Buhen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirgissa">Mirgissa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalfak">Shalfak</a>, Askut, Dabenarti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a>, built within signalling distance of one another; most now lie beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Built largely of sun-dried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a> and shaped to the rock, the fort is an example of the terrain type of Middle Kingdom fortress. Inside, stone-paved streets lead between large granaries, buildings thought to have been an administrative centre, a governor's house and barracks planned like the small state-built houses of other Middle Kingdom settlements. Many buildings were altered over time, and the pottery from its dumps points to occupation into the very late Middle Kingdom or the very beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>, but not beyond.</p><p>A massive boundary wall runs several hundred metres south towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a> fortress, and the largest of the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> buildings on the island, a rectangular structure called the Campaign Palace, was once thought to house the king on campaign, a reading with little evidence behind it.</p><p>Seal impressions, mud stamps, stelae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> fragments show how closely the fort's administration was documented. A near-duplicate of the boundary stela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a>, which claims his victory over a Nubian army in words that may be as much propaganda as record, was found here and is now in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Noel F. Wheeler excavated the site in 1924 under the nominal supervision of George Andrew Reisner, and Dows Dunham published the results and made the first map. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> was built the fort was thought to be under water, until Derek Welsby found it again. Since 2012 the Uronarti Regional Archaeology Project of Laurel Bestock and Christian Knoblauch has studied it, discovering Site FC, a settlement of at least 25 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> huts some 250 m south of the walls that was probably in use during the fort's early phase.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uronarti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A chain of mud-brick fortresses with bastions, ditches and loopholed walls, including Buhen, Mirgissa, <b>Uronarti</b>, Shalfak, Semna and Kumma, controlled the river and the desert routes and watched the growing kingdom of Kerma further south; the Semna Dispatches, copies of reports from these forts, record their patrols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li></ul></section>
